  #block(breakable: false)[
  == Complete Solutions

  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sokoto, founded by Muhammad Bello as the seat of the caliphate. Kano, Katsina and Zaria were emirates within it.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Mai ruled Kanem-Borno. Alaafin and Oba are Yoruba titles, and Emir is the title of a ruler within the Sokoto Caliphate.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Oba of Benin. The Mai ruled Kanem-Borno, the Emir an emirate of the caliphate, and the Bashorun headed the Oyomesi at Oyo.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Benin bronzes are the country's best-known pre-colonial art, produced by the guild of bronze casters at the Oba's court.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Oba governed with a council of chiefs whose consent mattered. The Alkali and the emirate council belong to the north, and the Oyomesi to Oyo.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Ozo title. The Oyomesi are Yoruba chiefs, the Alkali are judges under Sharia, and the Mai ruled Kanem-Borno.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Umuada, the daughters of the lineage, mediated in disputes and protected women's interests. The Iyalode is Yoruba.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The village group was the largest stable political unit in much of Igboland, linking villages through kinship, markets and oracles rather than through a king.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The jizya was the poll tax on protected non-Muslims under Islamic law. Zakat was levied on Muslims, and kharaj on land.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The desert routes linked the Sudanic states to North Africa, exchanging gold, slaves, leather and salt for horses, textiles and manufactured goods.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Oyo was the seat of the Alaafin. Ife is the spiritual home of the Yoruba, and Ibadan and Abeokuta rose later as warrior and missionary towns.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Oracles such as Ibini Ukpabi at Arochukwu had inter-village authority, which is why they could settle disputes that no single village court could.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The emir governed under Sharia and was the political and religious head of the emirate, advised by a council and answerable to the caliph.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The emirates and Yoruba kingdoms were centralised; the Igbo village republics and many Middle Belt communities were non-centralised. The classification is the point of Chapter 1.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Agriculture supported the villages, with market trade and river commerce. Mining and export manufacturing belong to the colonial and later economy.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The consulate was a trading and anti-slavery post, and its jurisdiction extended to the coastal trade before annexation.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The punitive expedition deposed the Oba and looted the palace, which is how the Benin bronzes entered European collections.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1903, at the battle of Sokoto, after which the caliphate's territory became the Northern Protectorate. 1900 is the transfer of the Royal Niger Company's territory.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lugard took office in 1900 and later became Governor-General after the 1914 amalgamation.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The expedition destroyed the Arochukwu oracle's influence, which had blocked British control of the eastern trade routes.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The British had to adjust the system to the Yoruba structure of Obas and councils, whose authority was more limited than that of an emir.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Native courts decided cases under customary and Islamic law, which is how indirect rule administered justice at the local level.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The 1917 ordinance introduced direct taxation in the south, and the attempt to apply it to women in the east produced the 1929 revolt.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cocoa from the west, groundnut from the north, palm produce from the east and cotton in several areas formed the export economy.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The chiefs governed at the base of the pyramid under the supervision of British officers, which is the essence of indirect rule.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The amalgamation created one administration under a Governor-General while keeping separate lieutenant-governors for the north and south.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Direct taxation, collected through the native authorities, financed the northern administration. Oil and company tax belong to the later economy.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The protesters attacked warrant chiefs' houses and native authority buildings, and the grievance was taxation and the chiefs' abuse of power.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Independence in 1960 ended colonial rule. The 1963 republic changed the head of state, and the 1966 coup ended the First Republic.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The north and south were administered separately, and the regions inherited those administrations in 1954 — the structure that federalism had to accommodate.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Clifford, 1922. It created elected seats for Lagos and Calabar, the first elections in the country.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    North, West and East, with the Cameroons attached to the East. The twelve states came in 1967 and the FCT in 1976.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Macpherson, 1951, created a Council of Ministers with Nigerian members holding portfolios.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lyttleton, 1954, divided power between the federal government and the regions, which is why it is the federal settlement.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The AG, led by Awolowo. The NCNC was 1944, the NNDP 1923 and the NEPU 1950.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    NEPU appealed to the talakawa, the common people of the north, against the emirs and the NPC establishment.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lagos. It contested the three Lagos seats created by the Clifford Constitution and was not a national party.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The strike showed that the colonial economy could be brought to a halt by organised workers, and it strengthened the nationalist movement.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Enahoro's motion split the House along regional lines, and the political tour that followed produced the Kano riots.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The conference settled the federal structure that the Lyttleton Constitution of 1954 enacted.
  ]

  #sol-item(41, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    December 1959. The new House met in 1960, and independence followed on 1 October 1960.
  ]

  #sol-item(42, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The NPC sought safeguards for the north, including a strong regional government and a fair share of seats, before accepting independence.
  ]

  #sol-item(43, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The 1960 Constitution was parliamentary, with a Prime Minister responsible to the House of Representatives and a Governor-General representing the Queen.
  ]

  #sol-item(44, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Queen was replaced by a Nigerian President, while the parliamentary system and the regions remained.
  ]

  #sol-item(45, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The 1961 plebiscite sent the Southern Cameroons to Cameroon, while the Northern Cameroons voted to remain in Nigeria.
  ]

  #sol-item(46, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Covering the field: once the federation legislates, a state law on the same matter yields to the extent of inconsistency.
  ]

  #sol-item(47, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Exclusive means federal only. The concurrent list is shared, and the residual list belongs to the states.
  ]

  #sol-item(48, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Residual matters are those not named in the exclusive or concurrent lists, and they fall to the states. Police, defence and citizenship are exclusive.
  ]

  #sol-item(49, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Mid-West was carved out of the west in 1963, giving four regions. The FCT and the twelve states came later.
  ]

  #sol-item(50, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Twelve, when the regions were abolished. Nineteen came in 1976, twenty-one in 1987 and thirty in 1991.
  ]

  #sol-item(51, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Thirty states in 1991, and six more in 1996 brought the total to thirty-six.
  ]

  #sol-item(52, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The FCT has one senator and representation in the House, but no governor: it is administered by a minister.
  ]

  #sol-item(53, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Inter-governmental disputes go directly to the Supreme Court, which is one of the defining features of a federation.
  ]

  #sol-item(54, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One constitution, a bicameral federal legislature, a Supreme Court and a division of powers. States have no separate constitutions and no right of secession.
  ]

  #sol-item(55, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The local government share comes from the Federation Account, which is why FAAC distributes to the third tier as well as the first two.
  ]

  #sol-item(56, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Section 14 requires that the composition of federal institutions reflect Nigeria's diversity. Section 4 is legislative power, section 8 state creation and section 42 freedom from discrimination.
  ]

  #sol-item(57, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The minimum-cost argument gives each state a basic share, which favours the smaller and poorer units.
  ]

  #sol-item(58, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The list itself is the source of the restriction, and a state law on an exclusive matter is void for inconsistency with the Constitution.
  ]

  #sol-item(59, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Smaller and more numerous states depend on the centre for revenue, which shifts the balance of the federation towards the federal government.
  ]

  #sol-item(60, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Fiscal federalism is the revenue and expenditure side of the federal bargain: who taxes, who shares and on what principle.
  ]

  #sol-item(61, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nzeogwu led the group of young officers. Gowon came to power after the July counter-coup, and Murtala and Babangida in 1975 and 1985.
  ]

  #sol-item(62, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ironsi was killed at Ibadan. Balewa and Bello had been killed in the January coup.
  ]

  #sol-item(63, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    July 1967, after the failure of the Aburi Accord and the declaration of Biafra. It ended in January 1970.
  ]

  #sol-item(64, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Biafran surrender in January 1970 ended the war, and Gowon followed it with the “no victor, no vanquished” policy.
  ]

  #sol-item(65, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Gowon was in Kampala at the OAU summit, which is one reason the coup was bloodless.
  ]

  #sol-item(66, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Obasanjo handed over to Shagari on 1 October 1979, beginning the Second Republic.
  ]

  #sol-item(67, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Buhari's coup of 31 December 1983 ended the Second Republic and began another military period.
  ]

  #sol-item(68, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Babangida introduced SAP in 1986. Buhari's programme was WAI, and Abacha's government is associated with the 1995 Constitution.
  ]

  #sol-item(69, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Babangida annulled the election in June 1993 and later handed power to an interim government in August.
  ]

  #sol-item(70, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    August to November 1993, when Abacha took over. It is the shortest administration in the chapter.
  ]

  #sol-item(71, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    8 June 1998. Abubakar took over and ran the transition that produced the 1999 Constitution.
  ]

  #sol-item(72, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
